## Part 9: Animation and Motion Vision

### What Every Animation Communicates

The entry clip-path wipe communicates: this person is precise. Nothing fades. It reveals.

The product card FLIP communicates: you zoomed in, not navigated. The work is continuous.

The neural web electrical impulses communicate: skills connect and reinforce each other. They are not isolated line items.

The flow diagram pulses communicate: this is a live system. Data is moving right now.

The scroll velocity response communicates: this site knows why you are here and serves you accordingly.

If an animation does not answer "what does this communicate?" — it should not exist.

### The Quality Bar

The reference is not other developer portfolios. The reference is the best interactive work being produced — not "impressive for a PM's portfolio" but genuinely impressive, full stop.

Every animated element would be comfortable appearing on an Awwwards daily pickup. If it would not, rebuild it.

### Motion Principles

Things that appear arrive purposefully. Things that disappear leave decisively. Nothing lingers. Nothing is decorative movement.

Entrances: quick-out-slow-in. The object arrives with intention and settles.  
Exits: slow-in-quick-out. The object leaves cleanly.  
Interactive physics: spring. Objects feel like they have mass.  
Scroll-driven: linear scrub with GSAP. The visitor's scroll speed controls the animation speed directly.

All animations respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. Visitors who set this preference receive an excellent experience with no animation — instant state changes, full information. Not a degraded experience. A different, complete one.

### What Runs on Every Device

Clip-path reveals, counter animations, section heading reveals, dot grid, card hover state changes, terminal cursor blink, parallax at low intensity — all devices.

Three-dimensional card tilt, custom cursor, magnetic button pull, heavy WebGL, complex canvas animations — desktop with precision cursor only.

All mobile-specific implementations are designed as complete, excellent experiences in their own right — not as fallbacks that apologize for what is missing.

