## Part 10: Responsive Philosophy

The portfolio is rethought per device context, not resized. The question for every section is: what is the best experience for a person using this specific device in this specific context?

### Complete Redesigns Per Device

**Neural web → grouped tag cloud on small phones.** The force graph needs space. The tag cloud communicates the same information — categories, proficiency by size, color-coded domains — in a format appropriate for a 375px screen.

**Constellation / product grid → single-column full-width cards on mobile.** All information visible without hovering. Nothing hidden behind an interaction that touch devices cannot perform.

**The three-column product grid → two columns on tablet, one column on mobile.**

**AI chat 400×560px drawer → full-screen slide-up on mobile.** A small fixed drawer on a phone screen is not usable.

**The complex flow diagrams → vertically stacked node-and-connector lists on mobile.** Same information, different format.

**Custom cursor → absent on touch devices.** Not hidden. Genuinely not there.

**Three-dimensional tilt effects → absent on touch devices.** Touch cannot provide the cursor position data these require.

### Universal Requirements

Touch targets: 44px minimum height and width on every interactive element on touch devices.  
Full-height elements: `100dvh` — never `100vh`. Mobile browsers change visible height when toolbars show and hide.  
Fixed elements: safe area insets on all. Notch, Dynamic Island, Android navigation bars — respected on every fixed-position element.  
Virtual keyboard: chat and terminal input remain visible when keyboard is open.  
Hover information: every piece of information available on hover is also accessible via tap.

