## Part 13: Copy Standards

### The Rules

**Specific.** Real product names, real numbers, real constraints, real outcomes. If a sentence could describe any developer, rewrite it.

**Honest.** What went wrong. What was learned. What he would do differently. Portfolios that hide failures are not credible.

**His voice.** Not polished for LinkedIn. Not corporate. The way he explains things to a smart person who is genuinely curious.

### Never

"Passionate about" — never.  
"Results-driven" — never.  
"Team player" — never.  
"Eager to learn" — never, even though it is true.  
Skills as a standalone noun list — never. Only as context for a specific product.  
Any sentence that could belong to anyone — never.

### The Portfolio's Argument in One Sentence

"Promoted to Junior PM in two months. For building ten products nobody asked for."

Everything in this portfolio — every product, every document, every terminal command response, every AI assistant answer — is the evidence for that sentence.

